Another very promising game, gone with the blink of a finger…  I must
remember her fate in case I ever again encounter this situation.  Hope
this post helps me with that.

                                  * * *

Lara the human tourist stumbled into the dungeon purely by accident –
but since she hadn't been there yet, she decided to stay a bit and take
some photos.

Her camera turned out to be quite an important tool, as she very soon
found out that most creatures adapted to the rather dimly lit dungeon
couldn't locate her after a bright flash – and she had brought a
well-charged battery.  On the second level, she noticed two stairways
down and decided to check out where they led.

She first arrived at a brightly lit cave populated mainly with gnomes
and dwarfs.  Particularly the dwarfs had some interesting gear, which
fit her too (Lara had always been a small girl).  A pick-axe and a stack
of daggers from the same source made her feel quite confident, so she
left the cave for now and went further down the main dungeon.  A strange
ring that she found on the floor could make her fly in the air.  She
decided to keep it – such a gadget might come in handy later.

At the third level she actually discovered a bookshop.  Couldn't believe
it at first that there are actual stores in a dungeon.  The fourth level
had a liquor store – probably in good demand down here.  Lara checked
the prices in both.  Way too high for what she was used to; she didn't
yet buy anything but the very cheapest scroll.  It turned out to be an
interesting one and helped her identify the loot she had found so far.

Further down she found two altars, only one of them co-aligned.  Lara
can be quite a rebellious girl from time to time, so she first went to
the cross-aligned one and prayed there.  The clear liquid she used in
the prayer promptly became cursed, passing on the curse to all objects
it made contact with.  Which in turn gave her some interesting ideas…

The co-aligned altar helped to mollify The Lady again.  After some time
of sacrifice and prayer, Lara noticed a large hammer leaning behind the
altar, which sure hadn't been there before.  It had a label with the
name “Mjollnir” exactly in the place where the price tag can be found on
hammers in a store.  Lara gave it a chance and hit the next monster
coming along with it.  Sparks flew from the hammer as the poor creature
was reduced to half its size, and considerable damage could be noticed
at the floor design where it had just stood.  “Works”, she said
satisfied as she continued on her way. (Some time later, some more
sacrifices made her discover a very useful athame tagged “Magicbane” as
well.  And some scrolls made her hammer even more effective.)

Fitted out like that, Lara decided to pay the large cave another visit.
 It turned out to go quite deep.  She explored it until she reached a
kind of town, with two trees being tended there, far beneath the earth.
 Like most towns, it had a number of magazines with useful stuff in
them.  There was also another cross-aligned altar, but since there was a
priest taking care of it, she didn't dare anything funny there.

Another thing however puzzled her:  The town had a well-sized security
force, and its chief was carrying a saber seemingly made of pure silver!
 As soon as Lara saw this, she just ached to have it.  Using all of her
female wiles, she lured the watch captain downstairs.  Out of earshot
from his corps, she slaughtered him and took the beautiful weapon for
herself.  She then exercised a bit with it, and soon was able to hit
with hammer and saber at once.  A few more scrolls made this combo quite
deadly to anything she encountered.

Lara then continued exploring the main dungeon, finding a few levels
with boulders and pits that strangely reminded her of some Japanese
computer game.  She decided to play along and at the fourth such level
was rewarded with a beautiful glittering amulet.  “It sure is too soon
to be the Amulet of Yendor they have been talking about, isn't it…?” she
asked herself.  She then found out that the shiny accessory didn't just
glitter, but reliably reflected any kind of ray that happened to pass
her way.  Useful, indeed.

Speaking of rays, one of the wands she found in that section of the
dungeon could make a quite deadly one.  Lara actually was lucky that it
was still lying on the ground when she found it, as learned of its dark
purpose only afterwards.

Back up she went, and down again to explore the rest of the cave system
the gnomes had built.  On the way down, she found the famous
seven-league boots, which fit her slender feet perfectly.  The bottom
level, which was maze-like and very different in structure from the
rest, contained a large number of gems, some of which were looking quite
valuable.  She also found a number of books there, which together with
several more she had already discovered made her want to sit down and
read a bit.  So much loot was there in the mines that she had to make
the trip back up twice, even when putting it into a sack!

Next, Lara brought all her books to the bookshop and asked the
shopkeeper about their price.  He turned out to be too greedy to make
the deal worth it, but with his help she was still able to get an idea
about their relative worth.  Lara retreated into a small sealed-off
room, bolted the door and wrote “Do not disturb” on the floor, using the
special athame The Lady had granted her.  (That translates “Elbereth” in
the language that most, but not all dungeon inhabitants use.)  One of
the first books she leaved through contained detailed descriptions of
all the items that can be found in a dungeon; she used it to identify
the other books and read the ones that sounded useful.  However, in
order to use the newly-learned skills, she would have to get rid of her
bulky armor…

Lara descended deeper when suddenly her cell phone rang.  “Strange”, she
thought, “how can the signal pass through all that stone above me?”  The
connection sure was bad enough, but she figured that she should look for
some device on the current dungeon floor.  After exploring the level
thoroughly, she found something that closely resembled a Star Trek
transporter platform.  And the moment she stepped on it, indeed she
found herself transported away to her far-away home planet of Ankh-Morpok!

                                  * * *

And there is where she made her fatal mistake.  Now it is known that
quite a number of traps of every design can be found in a dungeon, but
none of them is as devious as the polymorph trap.  It suddenly makes you
appear just like one of the monsters that usually roam a dungeon.
Sometimes a large and fearsome one, sometimes a dull and weak little
creature.  If the new form is too different from the native one, the
process can also severely ruin your armor.

In Lara's case, she became a baby silver dragon.  “Fine”, she said to
herself, “I'll remain in this form just long enough to grow up, and then
I'll lay some beautiful silver eggs to get me some glittering pet
dragons!”  For not only is the dragon a strong and much-feared
adversary, but Lara was also lusting after its famous silver scales, “of
which could be made a fine garment indeed!  Much lighter than that bulky
metal I'm wearing now, yet strong and even more protective – and
certainly amongst the most fashionable in the whole world!”

Thus, greed is what finally sealed Lara's end.  She continued fighting
monsters just like before as they came at her, and her brain translated
its human commands into actions that the dragon-body could understand,
mainly “bite down hard”.  No problem at all until she encountered a
creature feared even more than all dragons together, the cockatrice…
Lara didn't even have time to think “SH*T” as she turned into a statue.

